{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgyZmHTyyUqNTVrk9Kwd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote all 8 test statistics to `iter_4/critique/test_stats/`. I ran each one once on the observed responses as a quick check. Each returns one finite float in under 0.02 s, well inside the 0.3 s-per-call budget. I didn't run the posterior-predictive harness; the pipeline does that and decides which statistics count as significant.\n\nMost of them aim at gaps the new terminal-streak change leaves open. Unless noted, each looks only at pairs where both sequences have the same number of switches.\n\n| Statistic | What it probes | Observed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `run_length_variety_equal_switch_terminal` | Last round's second significant finding (mixed run lengths look more random), now also requiring the two final runs to match so the new terminal penalty can't explain it | 0.392 |\n| `initial_streak_equal_switch` | Whether people also count a streak at the *start* against a sequence; the model penalises only the end | 0.358 |\n| `terminal_effect_participant_sd` | How much people differ in how much the ending matters; the model gives everyone the same terminal penalty | 0.269 |\n| `longest_run_location_equal_longest` | Whether the longest run counts against a sequence more at an edge (start or end) than in the middle, with longest-run length also matched | 0.383 |\n| `head_balance_equal_switch` | Whether people favour an even heads/tails count more than the model's running-tally cue predicts | 0.664 |\n| `lag2_repeat_equal_switch` | Whether stretches of strict alternation (HTHT) count against a sequence | 0.500 (on 400 trials) |\n| `short_length_item_extremity` | How strongly people agree on each pair at lengths 2–5, against the model's length-scaled sensitivity | 0.214 |\n| `long_item_extremity` | The same agreement strength for length-8 pairs | 0.221 |\n\n`lag2_repeat_equal_switch` came out at exactly 0.5. I checked that this isn't an empty-subset fallback: 400 trials qualify, so on these data people show no preference either way.\n\nThe 0.358 for `initial_streak_equal_switch` means people chose the sequence with the shorter opening run only 36% of the time. If the replicates also show this, people prefer a sequence that opens on a streak, the opposite of the start-penalty idea the statistic was written to test. Whether that is a real discrepancy depends on the harness's null mean."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":573,"cache_read_input_tokens":52134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":573,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e5e14dda-5715-43af-8fc7-e09fde4f850d","uuid":"60167d31-15e7-454a-806d-739cb4b24f91","timestamp":"2026-10-04T09:40:30.922Z","request_id":"req_011CfgyZkruMbrzWwVxNzNm3"}
